Elys Savan is remembered in Rakuen as the architect-symbol of cooperative waterworks before Ashfall collapse.
Her cistern program embodied abundance planning, distributed stewardship, and southern municipal self-reliance.
When ash contamination turned those channels toxic, her works became the era's most cited lesson in infrastructure vulnerability.
Rakuen memorial culture preserves Savan as both civic ideal and warning against planning for only fair-weather conditions.
